(Bug #110's disambiguation question: LANGUAGE.md:691 rule 3 says a
 single-quoted token holding exactly one character is a character literal
 "no context-sensitivity" - `'A'` never becomes a name, in a slot or
 anywhere else, and a one-letter quoted NAME does not exist ("Write x, not
 'x'"). So `Set byte N of <buffer> to 'A'` (byte position) must go on
 emitting a character literal untouched, and `{'A'}` (slot position) must
 ALSO read `'A'` as the character literal 65, exactly like bare `Print
 'A'.` does outside any slot - not as an attempt to resolve a variable
 named A.)

a buffer called buf is "Hello".
Set byte 1 of buf to 'J'.
Print buf.

Print 'A'.
Print "{'A'}".
